From 0216672911cc18221178bdae727cd3f7c83a36e7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dimitri John Ledkov <dimitri.ledkov@surgut.co.uk>
Date: Tue, 24 Nov 2026 19:17:35 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] rsa: set OAEP digest, before padding mode with OpenSSL

Given

    OAEP = padding.OAEP(mgf=padding.MGF1(hashes.SHA256()), algorithm=hashes.SHA256(), label=None)

Pyca currently sets OAEP padding mode, then OAEP MD, and then MGF1 in
that order for all backends.

RSA OAEP RFC defaults to SHA-1 for OAEP MD, and hash in
MGF1. BoringSSL, LibreSSL, AWS-LC and OpenSSl 1.x all require padding
mode OAEP to be set before setting MD & MGF1 digests.

OpenSSL 3.x+ has a one-shot context API to set all OAEP params in one
call, and preffers to set MD digest, before setting the OAEP padding
mode. Otherwise, provider does a redundant implicit SHA-1 digest
fetch, if OAEP MD is not yet set, when OAEP mode is configured. If one
plans on using SHA256, a redundant SHA-1 digest fetch should be fairly
quick and harmless.

Unless, you are stuck with a validated provider developed in 2025, and
put in production in 2026, which doesn't offer SHA-1 digest. And the
above sequence, results in failure to configure OAEP context and lots
of sadness. This typically doesn't affect code coded for OpenSSL
provider/param APIs, as they usually use PARAM array to set all
security parameters in one shot. Which OpenSSL 3.x+ does in its
preffered order.

Maybe people shouldn't be using OAEP at all, and this misfeature
should also be fixed in OpenSSL (will submit a separate a bug about
that), but in the meantime reordering OAEP settings calls for OpenSSL
backend would help a lot.

This setting OAEP digests first ahead of OAEP padding mode for OpenSSL
backend, in most cases will be a no-op; or will safe on redudant
fetch; or will actually make things work with OpenSSLs configured at
runtime with providers lacking SHA-1.

I hope this patch is small enough enough / non-intrusive enough, to be
considered for merging.
---
 CHANGELOG.rst               |  4 ++++
 src/rust/src/backend/rsa.rs | 17 +++++++++++++++++
 2 files changed, 21 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/rust/src/backend/rsa.rs b/src/rust/src/backend/rsa.rs
index 3b259b4d0..56d70d815 100644
--- a/src/rust/src/backend/rsa.rs
+++ b/src/rust/src/backend/rsa.rs
@@ -128,6 +128,16 @@ fn setup_encryption_ctx(
         ));
     };
 
+    // LibreSSL, BoringSSL and AWS-LC reject the OAEP digests and label until
+    // the padding is OAEP. OpenSSL 3 accepts them first; there the padding is
+    // set after them, below. With OpenSSL setting digests first, can avoid
+    // implicit redundant SHA-1 fetch. And it can also prevent runtime failure
+    // when SHA-1 is blocked for compliance reasons, and SHA-2 is intended
+    #[cfg(any(
+        CRYPTOGRAPHY_IS_LIBRESSL,
+        CRYPTOGRAPHY_IS_BORINGSSL,
+        CRYPTOGRAPHY_IS_AWSLC
+    ))]
     ctx.set_rsa_padding(padding_enum)?;
 
     if padding_enum == openssl::rsa::Padding::PKCS1_OAEP {
@@ -164,6 +174,13 @@ fn setup_encryption_ctx(
         }
     }
 
+    #[cfg(not(any(
+        CRYPTOGRAPHY_IS_LIBRESSL,
+        CRYPTOGRAPHY_IS_BORINGSSL,
+        CRYPTOGRAPHY_IS_AWSLC
+    )))]
+    ctx.set_rsa_padding(padding_enum)?;
+
     Ok(())
 }
 
